{"id":796,"date":"2020-11-05T12:04:39","date_gmt":"2020-11-05T20:04:39","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=796"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:32","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:32","slug":"random","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/random\/","title":{"rendered":"3D: Random"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166bd84d9\" name=\"6a22166bd84d9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the ways to use return functions in your CoDrone Mini programs is with random. This is almost exactly what it sounds like: after importing the random library, you can create a variable that will store a random number. This is what that will look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">random_number = randint(1, 10)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this case, the variable is called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">random_number<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and a number between 1 and 10 is being called, or used, later on in the program.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try it out now! Use the random library to print out a random integer from 1 to 10. Once you see that, adjust the ranges within the parentheses to get different random numbers.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from random import randint\r\n\r\nrandom_number = randint(1, 10)\r\n\r\nprint(random_number)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166bd8620\" name=\"6a22166bd8620\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random LED<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the ways you can see return values with your CoDrone Mini is through its LED and number randomization. Quick review from the LED lesson: <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">an LED produces color as a combination of red, green, and blue. All of these values range from 0 to 255 corresponding to the color&#8217;s intensity, with 255 being the highest.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this program, import the CoDrone Mini, time, and random libraries, and then create the drone object and pair to CoDrone Mini. Next, create a for loop that will run three times.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In the loop, create variables that will get a random number between 0 and 255 for red, blue, and green. Those three numbers are combined together to change the LED\u2019s color. For example, if your program returned RGB values of 13, 246, and 197, your CoDrone Mini\u2019s LED would turn aqua.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, have your drone take off, wait briefly, and then return the red, green, and blue random numbers with a brightness of 100. This should change the color of your drone\u2019s LED. After that, have the drone land.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try this program out, and once you see what happens, you can add more flight movements in between takeoff and landing!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\nfrom random import randint\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(3):\r\n\r\n    red = randint(0,255)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 blue = randint(0,255)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 green = randint(0,255)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(red,blue,green, 100)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166bd8703\" name=\"6a22166bd8703\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Number guessing program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You know that game that starts with \u201cI\u2019m thinking of a number\u2026\u201d and you have to guess which number the person is thinking of? You\u2019re going to play that with your drone!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing libraries (CoDrone, direction, time, and random), creating the drone object, and pairing, have your drone take off. Next, create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">throttle<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and set its value to 20.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">number<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that will store and return a number between 1 and 10. Next, create a while loop. Create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">guess<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that will prompt the user to input a number between 1 and 10, and will then store that value. Within the while loop, set up conditionals. If their guess is too low, the drone should throttle down. If their guess is too high, the drone should throttle up. If their guess is just right, the drone should do a flip. The program should also include a print statement for when the user guesses a number that is not between 1-10 and a break if they want to quit the program. Finally, the drone should land.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Direction\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\nfrom random import randint\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\r\nthrottle = 20\r\n\r\nnumber = randint(1, 10)\u00a0 # randomly choose a number between 1-10\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    guess = int(input(\"Guess a number from 1 - 10... or enter 0 to quit: \"))\u00a0 # get input from user\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if guess == 0:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    break\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess &gt; 10 or guess &lt; 0:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Number is not between 1-10. Try again.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess == number:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Perfect!\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.flip(Direction.FORWARD)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess &lt; number:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Too low!\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(-throttle)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess &gt; number:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Too High!\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(throttle)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166bd876e\" name=\"6a22166bd876e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Magic 8 Ball<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In case you&#8217;re not entirely sure what a Magic 8 Ball is, it&#8217;s a toy that predicts your future when you ask it yes or no questions. Responses can be positive, negative, or unsure.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Turn CDM into a Magic 8 ball! Come up with 4 answers for each of the following: yes answers, maybe answers, or no answers. Then use the <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">input()<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> function to ask the user for a question. The program will randomly select one of those answers, and change the LED to either green, yellow, or red, accompanied by a flight movement.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column][\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 5 Random function [vc_column_text]One of the ways to use return functions in your CoDrone Mini programs is with random. This is almost exactly what it sounds like: after&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/random\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1730,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":744,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2122,2134,2138],"class_list":["post-796","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-random","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/796","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":2,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/796\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2790,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/796\/revisions\/2790"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1730"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=796"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=796"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=796"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}